WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:04.170
If someone speaks a little bit of German
and gets complimented or Japanese,

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:07.380
they're very proud of themselves that
they were able to say anything at all

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:08.719
in those languages that they've learned.

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:10.500
Whereas with English,
that's not good enough.

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:13.719
With English, there's a feeling that I
have to be almost like a native speaker.

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:14.890
Hi, Jennifer.

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:15.680
Hi, Steve.

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:16.880
Thanks so much for having me.

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:17.700
Thank you.

00:00:17.709 --> 00:00:19.400
No, I'm looking forward to talking to you.

00:00:19.419 --> 00:00:21.089
You do very important work.

00:00:21.189 --> 00:00:26.949
You teach English and in particular
with English, you often hear about

00:00:26.959 --> 00:00:30.509
people who are afraid to speak or
think they don't speak very well.

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:33.494
And yet these are people who
have spent years and years.

00:00:34.654 --> 00:00:38.294
And so I ask myself, there's more
resources available for learning

00:00:38.294 --> 00:00:39.644
English than any other language.

00:00:39.655 --> 00:00:44.484
So why is it that these people
still feel not satisfied with their

00:00:44.484 --> 00:00:46.395
level in English, in your opinion?

00:00:46.844 --> 00:00:48.074
That's a great question.

00:00:48.115 --> 00:00:52.674
And a lot of it, I think, comes
down to their expectation.

00:00:53.034 --> 00:00:56.915
They have this expectation that
they're going to be able to communicate

00:00:56.915 --> 00:00:59.205
the same way as a native speaker.

00:00:59.415 --> 00:01:01.135
My students always compare...

00:01:01.590 --> 00:01:07.590
The way they speak in English to the
way I speak in English they are upset

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:13.040
because our their accent isn't the exact
same as mine or their vocabulary isn't

00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:20.199
Advanced as mine and obviously it's
just not a fair comparison And also I

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:24.100
find that the students learning English
a lot of them that I have they've been

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:30.555
learning English their entire lives but
throughout their lives learning English,

00:01:30.575 --> 00:01:34.685
they've been in these environments
where they're constantly assessed.

00:01:35.025 --> 00:01:37.605
They're constantly assessed
with language exams.

00:01:37.605 --> 00:01:42.324
They're constantly told they're making
mistakes and they've developed this, this

00:01:42.334 --> 00:01:48.804
fear of using their English or they expect
perfection because of how often they're

00:01:48.814 --> 00:01:52.915
assessed and then they don't realize
when they get into the professional

00:01:52.915 --> 00:01:59.890
world, the working world, or even social
environment that the language is not so

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:05.830
rigid and there's a lot more flexibility
with the language in the real world.

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:08.269
I think you raised a few
very good points there.

00:02:08.299 --> 00:02:11.860
First of all, it seems that
people have higher expectations of

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:15.880
themselves when it comes to English
than they do for other languages.

00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:20.730
So if someone speaks a little bit of
German and gets complimented or Japanese,

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:23.929
uh, they're very proud of themselves that
they were able to say anything at all

00:02:23.929 --> 00:02:25.279
in those languages that they've learned.

00:02:25.599 --> 00:02:27.049
Whereas with English,
that's not good enough.

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:29.010
With English, there's a
feeling that I have to be.

00:02:29.625 --> 00:02:33.845
Almost like a native speaker and of
course, that's simply not possible.

00:02:34.205 --> 00:02:40.674
That's so true and also I find that my
students, they also compare how they

00:02:40.675 --> 00:02:46.275
speak in English to how they speak in
their own native language, which also is

00:02:46.275 --> 00:02:51.195
not a fair comparison obviously and this
creates a lot of anxiety in them because

00:02:51.195 --> 00:02:56.345
they think about how they would answer a
question in their native language and then

00:02:56.355 --> 00:03:00.655
when they go to answer that question in
English, They struggle a little bit more.

00:03:00.665 --> 00:03:03.095
The words don't flow as easily.

00:03:03.155 --> 00:03:08.255
And they see this as a huge problem and
they see this as, Well, I'm not fluent.

00:03:08.264 --> 00:03:09.515
I'll never progress.

00:03:09.535 --> 00:03:11.385
My English is terrible.

00:03:11.634 --> 00:03:15.475
But, of course, they can't
compare it to a native speaker.

00:03:15.504 --> 00:03:19.355
And they can't compare it to how
they speak in their own language.

00:03:19.929 --> 00:03:20.759
Oh, absolutely.

00:03:20.759 --> 00:03:25.289
I mean, when you consider how much we
have read and how much we have listened

00:03:25.369 --> 00:03:29.269
to our native language, so the brain has
so much experience with the language.

00:03:29.329 --> 00:03:31.399
So that's, again, why I emphasize input.

00:03:31.409 --> 00:03:34.789
You can't get to that level of
input experience with the language,

00:03:35.009 --> 00:03:38.789
but the more you invest in
getting exposure to the language.

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:43.950
The, the more weapons you will have when
you go to speak, have you had success

00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:48.239
stories where people have come to you and,
and obviously were struggling and were you

00:03:48.239 --> 00:03:50.269
able to find ways to help them improve?

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:54.240
Yes, thankfully I have had
success with many students.

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:58.210
So first I had to think about all
the mistakes that students were

00:03:58.210 --> 00:04:01.770
making and what was really preventing
them from reaching their goal.

00:04:01.770 --> 00:04:04.920
Again, their goal is just
communicating in English.

00:04:04.990 --> 00:04:08.030
I realized that there were really
two things we needed to do.

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:12.590
We had to fix their mindset
because they had to get over that.

00:04:12.685 --> 00:04:17.845
fear of making mistakes, that fear of
being judged, how critical they are

00:04:17.855 --> 00:04:19.625
of themselves when they're speaking.

00:04:19.945 --> 00:04:26.075
So addressing those and just helping them
relax and have fun speaking and just see

00:04:26.075 --> 00:04:31.085
it in a different way really helped them
to progress with their speaking skills.

00:04:31.305 --> 00:04:35.795
And for the more technical language
skills, vocabulary, grammar

00:04:35.815 --> 00:04:39.095
expressions, it was by helping them.

00:04:39.624 --> 00:04:43.094
Get content, get input
that they really enjoyed.

00:04:43.354 --> 00:04:49.494
So for my students, I help them learn
with TV shows, YouTube videos, movies,

00:04:49.505 --> 00:04:52.075
newspaper articles, short stories.

00:04:52.094 --> 00:04:54.764
And they enjoy learning with this content.

00:04:54.784 --> 00:04:57.005
And it's natural content.

00:04:57.015 --> 00:05:00.494
It teaches you how to use the
language in the real world.

00:05:00.705 --> 00:05:02.994
There again, I think two
important points that you made.

00:05:03.164 --> 00:05:06.104
The greater the variety of input,
the more comfortable you're

00:05:06.104 --> 00:05:07.044
going to be in the language.

00:05:07.044 --> 00:05:10.765
But, uh, you know, more than anything
else, relax and enjoy the process.

00:05:11.034 --> 00:05:16.615
So I will leave a link to your YouTube
channel in the description box.

00:05:16.685 --> 00:05:18.995
Tell us again where people can find you.

00:05:19.205 --> 00:05:23.225
Well, if you're learning English as
a second language, then you can find

00:05:23.225 --> 00:05:26.585
me on YouTube at jforestenglish.

00:05:26.675 --> 00:05:31.094
Well, I encourage people to go look
for you and to enjoy the process.

00:05:31.204 --> 00:05:32.534
Thank you very much, Jennifer.

00:05:32.534 --> 00:05:33.914
I enjoyed this discussion very much.

00:05:33.914 --> 00:05:34.784
Thank you so much, Steve.

00:05:34.784 --> 00:05:35.704
It's been a pleasure.

